     ``不要用于``, ``排除``, ``勿用于``.  Limit (named in the detail line):
     a vacuous "Never use this skill." passes and a real exclusion with no
     marker fails — it is a vocabulary test, not semantics.
+trigger.description_no_xml_tag  S003 (corpus/SOURCES.md): the parsed
+    ``description`` (unquoted, folded) contains no XML 1.0 tag.  A tag is
+    ``</name>``, ``<name ...>`` or ``<name/>``, ``name`` an XML Name (the 1.0
+    NameStartChar/NameChar ranges, so ``<名前>`` and ``<ns:x>`` count); an
+    attribute is ``name="v"`` or ``name='v'``, ``v`` free of ``<`` and of its
+    own quote.  The scan covers the whole value: a backticked span is NOT
+    exempt (the live ``<skills>`` sits in backticks), and a tag-shaped token
+    inside ``<!-- -->`` or CDATA is a tag.  NOT tags: ``a < b``, ``a <= b``,
+    ``a<=b``, ``<3``, ``<- x`` (no NameStartChar after ``<``); autolinks
+    ``<https://…>``, ``<mailto:…>``, ``<a@b.c>`` (``/`` and ``@`` are not
+    NameChars); a bare ``<!…>`` or ``<?…?>`` with no tag inside; escaped
+    ``&lt;x&gt;``; full-width ``＜x＞``; a valueless attribute ``<b c>``.  FAIL
+    names every token with its 0-based character offset in the parsed value.
+    ``evaluated`` = 1; a missing, empty or unparseable description FAILs
+    with 0.
 trigger.line_budget             the box binds the threshold: the first
     integer in the checklist box whose text matches ``file … <int> …
     line(s)`` is the limit (the live box reads ``File ≤ 600 lines``); when
@@ -195,6 +210,10 @@ DOCUMENTED LIMITS (what this instrument proves and what it never proves):
   - anti-trigger is a vocabulary test over the OBSERVED bilingual marker
     set — an unlisted exclusion phrasing FAILs unearned, a marker-bearing
     non-exclusion (or a vacuous "Never use this skill.") passes unearned;
+  - the XML-tag rule reads only the parsed ``description``: ``x<y>z`` FAILs
+    because a name between two brackets is grammatically a tag (it fails
+    closed rather than guess intent), while ``<b c>`` and ``<b c=d>`` pass as
+    non-XML; XML character references and full-width brackets are text;
   - the command vocabulary is a ceiling, named not hidden: a command
     outside the declared set used *inline* is invisible; inside a
     shell-fenced block every segment head is checked, so wrappers,
